summary_en.technical_approach:
Min-k Sampling dynamically chooses k by analyzing the local shape of the sorted logit sequence, explicitly locating the strongest “semantic cliff” and truncating there.
(1) Sort logits descending: l1>=l2>=...;
(2) Compute a logit dynamic range Rl (e.g., l1-l|V|, with ε for stability) to ensure invariance to linear transforms such as temperature scaling;
(3) Define a position-weighted relative decay over adjacent gaps (li-li+1), normalized by Rl and weighted by 1/i to emphasize head-region transitions and suppress tail noise;
(4) Select k* at the maximum weighted relative decay and sample only from the top-k* tokens (optionally applying temperature within this set).
Key properties: relative/normalized logit dynamics yield temperature invariance; focusing on local head structure avoids Top-nσ’s global-σ vulnerability; k adapts per step to the model’s confidence. Ablations show removing 1/i increases tail noise, while removing Rl normalization causes high-temperature collapse.
